arbon monoxide molecule consists of  a carbon atom triple bonded to an oxygen  atom carbon monoxide is not to be  confused with carbon dioxide the gaseous  molecule you exhale as a result of  metabolism carbon dioxide is made of a  carbon atom double bonded to two oxygen  both molecules share similar properties  they're both gaseous at room temperature  have no color no taste and no smell one  major difference though is that carbon  dioxide is not toxic and carbon monoxide  is carbon monoxide is toxic when it's  inhaled due to its effect on the  function of hemoglobin hemoglobin is a  vital protein embedded in your red blood  cells and is responsible for carrying  and releasing oxygen in your blood on  hemoglobin there are four protein  segments called hem groups with an iron  atom in the center when red blood cells  pass through a circulation in your lungs  inhaled oxygen diffuses into the  bloodstream and encounters the  hemoglobin protein oxygen then by binds  to each heme group resulting in four  oxygen molecules to one hemoglobin  protein once oxygen is bound to  hemoglobin it's renamed to oxyhemoglobin  red blood cells transport the  oxyhemoglobin throughout the body to  tissues that require oxygen oxygen then  leaves heme groups when hemoglobin is  exposed to blood that is somewhat acidic  blood becomes acidic when there is high  concentrations of carbon dioxide in the  blood gaseous carbon dioxide reacts with  water forming carbonic acid and makes  the blood slightly acidic high  concentrations of carbon dioxide in  blood is due to increased metabolism  such as exercise or stress the more  carbon dioxide you generate the more  oxygen gets released into the blood the  system works perfectly until carbon  monoxide is present then things go  horribly wrong the degree of how strong  or weak two molecules bind is called  Affinity Oxygen's affinity for  hemoglobin is measured in millimeters of  mercury and is about 27 units this is a  fair really weak Affinity but  intentionally because oxygen has to be  able to leave hemoglobin at a moment's  notice carbon monoxide's affinity for  hemoglobin is much higher at 65  millimeters of mercury or 240 times  stronger than oxygen this stronger  Affinity keeps carbon monoxide bound to  hemoglobin much more tightly than oxygen  and it doesn't let go for about five  hours  carbon monoxide's first effect on the  body is when it binds to a heme group on  hemoglobin once bounded it takes the  place of an oxygen molecule allowing  only three oxygen to bind instead of  four it should also be noted when carbon  monoxide binds to hemoglobin hemoglobin  is renamed to carboxyhemoglobin due to  its different properties one notable  property is that the molecule itself  produces a bright cherry red color which  is a Telltale sign of carbon monoxide  poisoning one who has inhaled a  significant amount of carbon monoxide  will have a cherry red Hue to their skin  this is because a large amount of  hemoglobin in their blood has been  converted to carboxyhemoglobin  unfortunately by the time the skin has  identifiable color the victim is usually  deceased due to the high concentrations  in the blood taking the place of oxygen  on hemoglobin isn't the only thing  carbon monoxide does it also affects the  other oxygen indirectly as well the  charge on carbon monoxide stabilizes the  iron ion in the center of the protein  when iron becomes stabilized it changes  the physical shape of the heme groups as  a result the oxygen molecules stay fixed  to the heme groups as long as carbon  monoxide is also present in essence  oxygen cannot be used by the body as a  result the oxygen cannot be released  into the blood causing asphyxiation or  deprivation of oxygen early symptoms of  asphyxiation first manifest as flu-like  symptoms such as headache weakness and  dizziness as more carbon monoxide is  inhaled symptoms progress to chest pain  vomiting confusion irregular heartbeat  and loss of consciousness once  unconsciousness is reached all that's  left is for the brain to shut down and  then it's over  depending on the concentration of carbon  monoxide fatal effects may happen within  minutes or hours the low end of fatal  concentrations begin at 3 200 parts per  million and can cause death within 30  minutes at 12 800 parts per million  fatal effects can occur within three  minutes or less in contrast lethal  concentrations of carbon dioxide is  about 40 000 parts per million because  carbon dioxide is not toxic there just  has to be enough to displace available  oxygen  whereas carbon monoxide directly shuts  down oxygen distribution carbon monoxide  is also less dense than oxygen and  carbon dioxide meaning it will diffuse  throughout a room fairly well instead of  settling near the ground we know how  carbon monoxide poisoning works but  where does it come from  carbon monoxide is produced when there  is a low amount of oxygen during a  combustion reaction in combustion  reactions hydrocarbons are burned in the  presence of oxygen resulting in the  formation of water and carbon dioxide if  there's not enough oxygen present there  won't be enough to make carbon dioxide  instead the reaction will still proceed  but only one oxygen will bind to carbon  instead of two making water and carbon  monoxide low oxygen combustion reactions  typically occur when something is  burning in a closed space where there is  a low amount of oxygen available the  most common occurrence of this is an  internal combustion engine being run in  a closed space such as a garage carbon  monoxide can also occur in your own home  when furnace ventilation gets blocked  which is a common occurrence in the  winter charcoal is also known to release  significant amounts of carbon monoxide  during its combustion and was famously  used as a means of by K-pop singer Chung  Young there is another occurrence of  carbon monoxide poisoning in everyday  life that millions of people partake in  smoking inhaling smoke from both burning  tobacco and cannabis delivers carbon  monoxide directly into the blood whether  it's rolled in paper or in a pipe the  combustion does not burn with sufficient  oxygen and therefore produces carbon  monoxide there is enough carbon monoxide  in cigarette and cannabis smoke to  develop mild carbon monoxide poisoning  depending on how much is smoked per day  in a study from the Spanish clinical  Journal 228 smokers were tested for  carbon monoxide presence in their blood  the average non-smoker has a one percent  carbon monoxide concentration in their  blood from biological production and  small concentrations in the air in  contrast smokers who had between 1 in 10  cigarettes per day had a blood carbon  monoxide concentration of 5.2 percent  those who smoked more than 20 cigarettes  per day had a 9 9.6 percent  concentration blood levels of 10 percent  or higher is defined as carbon monoxide  poisoning symptoms that chronic smokers  have such as fatigue headache shortness  of breath and confusion are more likely  than not the effects of mild carbon  monoxide poisoning in fact chronic  smokers have more hemoglobin in their  blood than a non-smoker as their body  has to make more to compensate for  hemoglobin that's been inactivated  despite how dangerous carbon monoxide  poisoning is if caught quickly it can be  treated patients are given oxygen  through breathing masks or if necessary  placed in hyperbaric oxygen Chambers  where they breathe nearly pure oxygen  high concentrations of oxygen cause  carbon monoxide to unbind from the heme  groups much faster reducing the time  it's attached from five hours to about  an hour and a half  with carbon monoxide Unbound oxygen can  bind and unbind from hemoglobin as  normal  sometimes science can be seen as  difficult foreign and even boring yet  there are times when science grabs our  attention and draws Us in yearning for  more answers often what draws us Center  topics that are dark Macabre or  terrifying it's with the intention of  this video that you too were drawn in  and sought to have your questions  answered thanks for watching Dark  science  [Music]
 

